1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,048
Sections seven and eight use the same assessment framework as the
preceding sessions: establish the individual baseline, identify the
new change and choose a specific modification.

2
00:00:12,048 --> 00:00:23,184
The task is not to memorize a list of diseases associated with
obesity. Explain which mechanism could affect this patient, what
evidence you seek and how that changes care.

3
00:00:23,184 --> 00:00:34,968
Airway preparation, safe positioning and an extraction plan should
start early. Waiting until a patient deteriorates can make a
previously manageable access problem much harder.

4
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:47,736
The movement principles here do not certify use of a lifting device or
replace its instructions. Actual equipment, local procedures, team
training and the clinical situation determine the safe method.

5
00:00:47,736 --> 00:00:59,472
Use neutral, person-centered language and ask how the patient prefers
to be addressed. Avoid jokes, blame or conversations about the patient
as though they were not present.

6
00:00:59,472 --> 00:01:10,416
Explain that additional equipment or personnel are being requested to
support safe care. Do not describe the patient as the problem or
promise that a difficult move will be effortless.

7
00:01:10,416 --> 00:01:22,584
Size does not tell you whether someone can walk, understands a
decision or has a particular illness. Ask, observe and assess instead
of substituting a stereotype for evidence.

8
00:01:22,584 --> 00:01:34,200
Expose only what is needed, provide appropriate covering and seek
permission for sensitive examination. Urgent care can still include a
clear explanation and respectful handling.

9
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:45,192
Ask how the patient normally transfers and what assistance or devices
are used. A usual independent transfer does not prove that it remains
safe during the present illness.

10
00:01:45,192 --> 00:01:56,736
Clarify usual breathing, tolerated sleeping position and prescribed
respiratory support. Ask what is different today, including new
inability to lie back or a change in alertness.

11
00:01:56,736 --> 00:02:10,848
Obtain the symptom timeline and associated findings without assuming
all dyspnea is caused by size. Cardiac, infectious, thromboembolic,
metabolic and drug-related illness can coexist.

12
00:02:10,848 --> 00:02:22,512
A patient may know which approach previously worked or caused pain.
Use that information in the plan while independently checking current
physiology, equipment and environmental safety.

13
00:02:22,512 --> 00:02:35,184
Additional chest-wall and abdominal loading can make expansion harder
and reduce resting lung volume. The effect varies between patients and
is not an identical physiological state in everyone.

14
00:02:35,184 --> 00:02:46,968
When the patient lies flat, abdominal contents may further limit
diaphragm movement. New orthopnea or worsening effort during
repositioning is a clinical finding to act on.

15
00:02:46,968 --> 00:02:59,952
Reduced reserve means oxygen levels may fall quickly when breathing
stops or an airway intervention interrupts ventilation. Anticipation
is useful, but it does not predict an exact safe apnea time.

16
00:02:59,952 --> 00:03:12,384
Assess actual ventilation rather than count a respiratory rate alone.
Chest movement, depth, air entry, responsiveness and the trend help
identify inadequate breathing.

17
00:03:12,384 --> 00:03:25,224
A saturation reading estimates oxygenation and does not directly
measure carbon dioxide or airway protection. Supplemental oxygen can
improve that number while ventilation remains inadequate.

18
00:03:25,224 --> 00:03:37,128
A patient who becomes increasingly drowsy with shallow breathing needs
urgent assessment even if a displayed saturation appears acceptable.
Do not let the monitor replace direct observation.

19
00:03:37,128 --> 00:03:50,880
Use capnography when available, appropriate and authorized,
recognizing its sampling and perfusion limitations. A single end-tidal
number is not identical to an arterial blood-gas diagnosis.

20
00:03:50,880 --> 00:04:03,672
After changing position or assisting breathing, reassess movement,
effort, responsiveness and reliable measurements. An intervention is
only useful if its actual effect is checked.

21
00:04:03,672 --> 00:04:14,640
Obstructive sleep apnea involves repeated upper-airway obstruction
during sleep. Ask whether it is diagnosed and whether the patient uses
prescribed positive-airway-pressure support.

22
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:27,120
Snoring, witnessed pauses and daytime sleepiness can be useful
history, but they do not establish the diagnosis by themselves. Not
every larger-bodied patient has sleep apnea.

23
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:36,912
New confusion, severe breathlessness or failure to remain awake should
not be labelled normal sleep apnea without assessing immediate threats
and other causes.

24
00:04:36,912 --> 00:04:50,904
A home device does not automatically provide appropriate emergency
ventilation. Check the current condition, actual equipment and
applicable pathway rather than simply place an unresponsive patient on
their usual mask.

25
00:04:50,904 --> 00:05:04,440
The ATS definition includes a body-mass index of at least thirty,
sleep-disordered breathing and awake resting arterial carbon dioxide
of at least forty-five millimeters of mercury after other causes are
excluded.

26
00:05:04,440 --> 00:05:18,552
That is a diagnostic framework, not a prehospital label applied from
appearance or a pulse-oximeter reading. Obesity, obstructive sleep
apnea and obesity hypoventilation syndrome are related but distinct.

27
00:05:18,552 --> 00:05:31,776
A normal-looking saturation does not establish normal carbon dioxide,
and suspected chronic disease does not explain away acute
deterioration. Other causes of hypoventilation still matter.

28
00:05:31,776 --> 00:05:44,760
For this lesson, the action is to recognize and support failing
breathing through the authorized pathway. Long-term testing, PAP
prescriptions and weight-management treatment are not being prescribed
here.

29
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:58,344
The school connects severe obesity with hypertension, increased
cardiac workload, ischemic disease and heart failure. These are
possible associated risks, not diagnoses guaranteed in every patient.

30
00:05:58,344 --> 00:06:14,328
Ask about new chest discomfort, breathlessness, exertional limitation,
swelling and the time course. Diabetes or other disease may alter
symptoms, but absence of classic pain does not exclude an acute
coronary problem.

31
00:06:14,328 --> 00:06:26,952
Difficulty seeing neck veins or hearing crackles does not prove their
absence or exclude failure. State the examination limitation and
combine other findings and appropriate monitoring.

32
00:06:26,952 --> 00:06:38,328
Do not give fluid simply because the examination is difficult or
assume all edema is new cardiac failure. The physiological problem and
authorized care pathway determine treatment.

33
00:06:38,328 --> 00:06:49,416
Shock can develop in a patient of any size. An unobtainable or
unreliable blood pressure is missing information, not proof of
adequate perfusion.

34
00:06:49,416 --> 00:07:01,224
Use responsiveness, peripheral findings, pulse, breathing and
appropriate repeated measurements together. A normal-range pressure
does not by itself rule out a developing emergency.

35
00:07:01,224 --> 00:07:12,984
Search for the plausible mechanism, including bleeding, infection,
cardiac dysfunction and obstructive causes. Size can complicate
detection without being the cause of the illness.

36
00:07:12,984 --> 00:07:25,680
Explain uncertainty during consultation or handover and repeat the
assessment after care or movement. Do not invent a reassuring normal
finding when the examination could not establish it.

37
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:36,288
Soft tissue, neck mobility and respiratory mechanics can make airway
management more difficult, but difficulty must be assessed rather than
assumed solely from weight.

38
00:07:36,288 --> 00:07:47,520
Bring suction and appropriate authorized airway equipment into reach.
Assign help early when positioning, mask seal or ventilation will
require more than one provider.

39
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:58,752
Choose the least invasive effective approach and verify its effect.
Oxygen delivery alone does not reopen every obstruction or provide
breaths to an apneic patient.

40
00:07:58,752 --> 00:08:11,664
Advanced airway procedures remain role- and situation-dependent.
Preparing for difficulty does not make an uncredentialed intervention
appropriate or justify delay in basic ventilation.

41
00:08:11,664 --> 00:08:24,768
The school ramped position elevates the head and upper torso with
stable support. The external ear canal and sternal notch are brought
toward the same horizontal level rather than forcing the neck into
flexion.

42
00:08:24,768 --> 00:08:36,672
Use equipment and supports that remain stable under the actual load.
Check that the adjustment improves airway access and breathing instead
of leaving the chin compressed toward the chest.

43
00:08:36,672 --> 00:08:48,480
An awake breathless patient may prefer a supported upright position.
The best position depends on airway protection, perfusion, injury
constraints and what can safely be maintained.

44
00:08:48,480 --> 00:09:01,128
Ramping for an airway is not a universal position for cardiac arrest
or every trauma. Use the applicable resuscitation and injury
requirements, and reassess the effect of any change.

45
00:09:01,128 --> 00:09:13,248
When breathing is inadequate, follow the appropriate airway and
assisted-ventilation pathway. Address patency and use a suitable
interface rather than simply increase oxygen flow.

46
00:09:13,248 --> 00:09:24,504
Two-provider mask ventilation can separate airway positioning and seal
from bag operation. Allocate tasks and observe whether each breath
produces useful chest movement.

47
00:09:24,504 --> 00:09:38,856
Excess force or speed can cause harm and does not solve every poor
seal or obstruction. If effect is poor, reassess the airway, device,
position and possible causes rather than repeatedly squeeze harder.

48
00:09:38,856 --> 00:09:52,080
Continue the appropriate ventilation rate, monitoring and escalation
for the clinical situation. This session adds positioning
considerations, not an obesity-specific replacement resuscitation
algorithm.

49
00:09:52,080 --> 00:10:05,280
The school stresses early oxygen preparation because reserve may be
limited. That should not be translated into permanent high-flow oxygen
for every patient regardless of oxygenation or clinical state.

50
00:10:05,280 --> 00:10:18,384
Use the applicable oxygen pathway, device and reliable measurements
while treating immediate threats. Severe hypoxemia or resuscitation
may require urgent rescue oxygen and ventilation.

51
00:10:18,384 --> 00:10:32,112
Do not apply a COPD-specific target merely because someone has
obesity, OSA or possible carbon-dioxide retention. Establish the
relevant clinical history and use the actual authorized pathway.

52
00:10:32,112 --> 00:10:45,192
Never withhold necessary support through fear of a simplified hypoxic-
drive story. Reassess consciousness and ventilation as well as
saturation and arrange the resources the patient needs.

53
00:10:45,192 --> 00:10:57,384
Choose a cuff whose stated range fits the measured arm. A cuff that is
too small can overestimate pressure; a poor fit or incorrect technique
can make a numerical result unreliable.

54
00:10:57,384 --> 00:11:09,672
Support the limb and use the appropriate measurement position and
device instructions. The biggest available cuff is not automatically
correct if it is applied poorly or outside its supported range.

55
00:11:09,672 --> 00:11:21,144
If a standard upper-arm measurement is not possible, follow the
approved alternative and record where and how it was obtained. Do not
quietly treat a different site as an identical measurement.

56
00:11:21,144 --> 00:11:33,744
Combine perfusion findings and repeated observations while seeking a
reliable value. Failed measurement is not a normal pressure, and a
corrected reading does not erase the clinical examination.

57
00:11:33,744 --> 00:11:44,640
Heart and lung sounds may be harder to hear through tissue or
background noise. Improve positioning, reduce noise and repeat
relevant areas with suitable equipment.

58
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:55,680
Do not use painful pressure or abandon the examination without
explanation. Ask about discomfort and obtain help when access or
positioning limits a useful assessment.

59
00:11:55,680 --> 00:12:10,320
Combine the examination with observed effort, chest movement, reliable
oxygenation, pulse, pressure and symptoms. No single normal-looking
finding excludes an important respiratory or cardiac emergency.

60
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:21,360
Document a sound as difficult to assess when that is true. Describing
an uncertain result as clear lungs can mislead the next clinician and
alter the treatment plan.

61
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:34,488
The school describes lower ECG amplitude in some larger-bodied
patients, but its phrase normal artifact is too reassuring. A
genuinely small signal is not the same thing as electrical noise.

62
00:12:34,488 --> 00:12:47,976
Check the actual acquisition process, including lead placement,
contact, motion and device settings. Correct a technical problem and
reassess without inventing a new calibration convention.

63
00:12:47,976 --> 00:13:01,536
Interpret the ECG with symptoms and perfusion, and seek the
appropriate review when findings are uncertain. Body habitus does not
independently explain every low-amplitude or abnormal tracing.

64
00:13:01,536 --> 00:13:14,832
A nondiagnostic ECG does not rule out acute coronary disease. Continue
the indicated clinical pathway and conveyance plan rather than dismiss
new chest or breathing symptoms as weight-related.

65
00:13:14,832 --> 00:13:29,904
Skin folds and areas beneath devices may hide injury or infection. In
a patient with relevant pain, fever, drainage or unexplained illness,
ask about these areas and perform a proportionate examination.

66
00:13:29,904 --> 00:13:42,216
Explain the reason, obtain appropriate permission, provide covering
and ask how the patient can help safely. Avoid dragging or lifting
tissue in a painful or damaging manner.

67
00:13:42,216 --> 00:13:57,528
Record objective findings such as location, redness, open areas,
drainage and tenderness. Odor alone does not identify an organism, and
a visible wound is not proof that it explains all systemic symptoms.

68
00:13:57,528 --> 00:14:09,168
Protect skin during transfer and communicate concerns. Do not perform
unapproved invasive wound treatment or delay urgent care to produce a
complete photographic inventory.

69
00:14:09,168 --> 00:14:20,568
Deeper veins and difficulty identifying landmarks may make access
harder. Begin by deciding whether access is indicated and whether it
changes the immediate treatment plan.

70
00:14:20,568 --> 00:14:34,560
The school mentions longer catheters, ultrasound and alternatives.
These are not interchangeable solutions or new permissions; the actual
device, procedure, operator training and role must support their use.

71
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:46,512
Follow attempt limits and escalation requirements. Do not repeatedly
puncture, guess an inaccessible landmark or use a needle length
selected only because the patient is large.

72
00:14:46,512 --> 00:14:59,424
Continue the primary care and conveyance plan while resources are
arranged. Vascular access is a means to indicated care, not a task
that must be completed before leaving every scene.

73
00:14:59,424 --> 00:15:10,296
Some medicines use weight, some use a fixed dose and some have a
ceiling. A larger patient does not automatically receive a
proportional increase in every drug.

74
00:15:10,296 --> 00:15:23,184
If a protocol specifies actual, ideal or adjusted weight, use that
defined method and an appropriate verified weight source. Do not
invent a substitution from a general obesity lecture.

75
00:15:23,184 --> 00:15:37,680
Confirm indication, product, concentration, route, maximum and
authorization before calculation. Estimating body size by eye is not a
reliable substitute for the required weight method.

76
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:48,408
Check the clinical response and adverse effects after permitted
treatment. The practical goal is correct patient-specific care, not a
general rule to give more medication.

77
00:15:48,408 --> 00:16:00,648
A movement assessment combines the patient's current clinical needs
with the physical task. A conscious patient may still be unsafe to
walk because of instability, injury or severe breathlessness.

78
00:16:00,648 --> 00:16:11,256
Define the actual transfer: from floor, chair or bed; across which
surfaces; through which route; and with what ongoing airway or
monitoring needs.

79
00:16:11,256 --> 00:16:24,000
Assess space, stairs, obstacles, weather and the destination loading
process. A device that fits beside a bed may not fit through the
planned doorway or onto the next platform.

80
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:36,864
Use appropriate controls and trained equipment rather than assume more
manual lifters make any task safe. If the plan cannot be performed
safely, change the plan and request the right resources.

81
00:16:36,864 --> 00:16:50,040
Invite the patient to describe a usual transfer and any painful or
ineffective previous methods. Participation should preserve autonomy,
not shift responsibility for an unsafe task onto them.

82
00:16:50,040 --> 00:17:01,416
A verbal assurance of being able to walk is not the whole assessment.
Check clinical suitability, observed function and the route, using the
approved staged method when appropriate.

83
00:17:01,416 --> 00:17:12,480
Do not force a walk test in a patient with serious cardiac,
respiratory or injury concerns. Use appropriate nonambulatory support
and additional help when indicated.

84
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:25,200
If unexpected assistance or unsafe effort is needed, stop and
reassess. The patient's wish to avoid embarrassment is important, but
it does not make an unstable transfer safe.

85
00:17:25,200 --> 00:17:36,000
Inspect the whole route, not only the first doorway. Consider turning
space, stair geometry and the room needed for operators to use the
chosen equipment correctly.

86
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:45,816
Check the surfaces and loading points the equipment will cross. Weight
limits and support requirements apply to every component, not just the
main stretcher.

87
00:17:45,816 --> 00:17:58,944
Plan how oxygen, monitoring, airway access and attachments remain
protected. A transfer that loses necessary ventilation or pulls out a
line is not successful merely because the patient moved.

88
00:17:58,944 --> 00:18:11,232
When the route or structure makes the task unsafe, seek appropriate
specialist resources and a safer alternative. Do not improvise
structural changes or unsupported lifting methods.

89
00:18:11,232 --> 00:18:21,720
Use the actual manufacturer rating and local procedure for every
device. A remembered number from another stretcher or a verbal guess
is not a capacity check.

90
00:18:21,720 --> 00:18:31,752
Fit matters as well as mass. Check width, limb support, securement,
pressure points and whether the patient can maintain the necessary
breathing position.

91
00:18:31,752 --> 00:18:45,408
A transfer sheet, lifting aid, stretcher and loading system have
different functions. Confirm compatibility and limits for the whole
sequence; one high-capacity item does not upgrade the others.

92
00:18:45,408 --> 00:18:58,920
Inspect condition, power and required accessories and use trained
operators. If the necessary equipment is absent, communicate the gap
and arrange a safe alternative instead of gambling on a trial lift.

93
00:18:58,920 --> 00:19:08,832
Identify a team leader and explain the sequence before movement. Every
team member should know who can stop the task and what signal means
stop immediately.

94
00:19:08,832 --> 00:19:21,648
Assign the patient communicator, airway or monitor responsibility,
equipment operators and attachment checks. Avoid asking one person to
perform incompatible jobs simultaneously.

95
00:19:21,648 --> 00:19:33,216
Invite concerns before the load moves and confirm readiness. A team
member who cannot see a hazard or reach a control needs the plan
adjusted, not pressure to continue.

96
00:19:33,216 --> 00:19:43,752
Use clear commands and controlled movement with the selected device.
More people acting independently can increase risk; coordination and
the correct equipment matter.

97
00:19:43,752 --> 00:19:55,440
Explain the next action to the patient and verify that the intended
breathing support and monitoring can continue. Check equipment setup,
route and team readiness before loading.

98
00:19:55,440 --> 00:20:07,416
Move in a controlled coordinated manner using the device instructions.
Friction-reducing aids can reduce shear and effort, but they are not
all lifting devices and must not be used as if they were.

99
00:20:07,416 --> 00:20:18,216
Observe the patient, not only the equipment. Protect limbs, skin and
attachments and maintain covering without obstructing necessary airway
access or monitoring.

100
00:20:18,216 --> 00:20:29,736
At the receiving surface, verify securement, pressure areas, position
and device connections. Recheck the important clinical findings before
calling the transfer complete.

101
00:20:29,736 --> 00:20:41,376
Stop the movement safely if the patient develops new respiratory
distress, severe pain or reduced responsiveness. Resume the primary
assessment and provide immediate support.

102
00:20:41,376 --> 00:20:52,008
Equipment instability, an uncertain load limit or a malfunction
requires a changed plan. Do not test an uncertain limit with the
patient as the load.

103
00:20:52,008 --> 00:21:02,904
An unexpected obstacle or unsafe operator position is sufficient
reason to pause. A partially completed transfer does not make the
remaining unsafe step acceptable.

104
00:21:02,904 --> 00:21:14,856
After stabilizing the situation, communicate what changed and request
the appropriate help. Document the actual reason and alternative,
without blaming the patient for the safety problem.

105
00:21:14,856 --> 00:21:26,688
Repeat breathing findings and confirm that the patient tolerates the
new position. A previously useful ramp can shift during transfer and
an oxygen connection can become ineffective.

106
00:21:26,688 --> 00:21:39,096
Check symptoms, responsiveness and relevant measurements against the
pre-move findings. An improved pulse-oximeter number alone is not
proof that the transfer caused no physiological harm.

107
00:21:39,096 --> 00:21:51,744
Look for painful pressure, trapped limbs, skin shear or disrupted
attachments and correct safe reversible problems. Ask the patient
directly about comfort and remaining concerns.

108
00:21:51,744 --> 00:22:03,264
Continue time-sensitive conveyance and explain adaptations at
handover. Equipment and movement details should support continuity,
not replace the clinical illness story.

109
00:22:03,264 --> 00:22:14,976
This is an original teaching example, not a school assessed scenario.
A fifty-eight-year-old reports worsening breathlessness over two days
and cannot tolerate lying back today.

110
00:22:14,976 --> 00:22:27,912
They report nocturnal CPAP and usually walk short distances
independently. Those facts describe useful history; they do not
establish the cause of this new illness or current walking safety.

111
00:22:27,912 --> 00:22:39,888
The route includes a narrow stair turn, and the available stretcher
capacity has not been verified. Unknown capacity remains unknown; do
not assume the equipment is suitable.

112
00:22:39,888 --> 00:22:49,416
State two simultaneous priorities: assess and support the clinical
problem, and arrange a safe movement solution before attempting
extraction.

113
00:22:49,416 --> 00:23:02,448
Assume the patient is alert but speaks in short phrases with increased
effort, a respiratory rate of thirty and pulse of one hundred twelve.
A reliable room-air saturation is eighty-nine percent.

114
00:23:02,448 --> 00:23:16,368
A pressure of one hundred seventy-six over one hundred two was
obtained with a visibly undersized cuff. Treat that reading as
questionable and seek a suitable measurement rather than declare a
hypertensive diagnosis.

115
00:23:16,368 --> 00:23:27,864
Lung sounds are difficult to assess in the noisy room and weight is
not verified. Neither missing finding should be turned into a normal
examination or a confident dose calculation.

116
00:23:27,864 --> 00:23:39,720
Prioritize breathing support and a focused cardiac, respiratory and
other-cause assessment. The history of CPAP does not prove that sleep
apnea explains this acute dyspnea.

117
00:23:39,720 --> 00:23:51,480
Maintain a supported position that helps breathing while providing
indicated authorized oxygen and airway care. Reassess the effect, and
arrange escalation for failing breathing.

118
00:23:51,480 --> 00:24:04,224
Continue relevant examination, measurements and ECG without delaying
essential care. Seek a useful symptom and medicine history while
acknowledging the uncertainty in the initial pressure.

119
00:24:04,224 --> 00:24:16,080
Do not ask this acutely breathless patient to negotiate the stairs
merely because they usually walk. Do not perform a trial lift with
equipment whose capacity and route fit remain unknown.

120
00:24:16,080 --> 00:24:28,392
Request the appropriate rated system, trained personnel and route
solution. Continue clinical care while preparation occurs and
communicate if urgency requires a revised coordinated plan.

121
00:24:28,392 --> 00:24:39,504
Now assume oxygen has increased the displayed saturation to ninety-
five percent, but the patient becomes drowsier with shallow and less
effective chest movement.

122
00:24:39,504 --> 00:24:50,616
That is not recovery merely because the oxygen number improved.
Reassess airway protection and ventilation immediately and check the
device and the actual clinical response.

123
00:24:50,616 --> 00:25:02,664
Provide the authorized ventilatory support and escalation the findings
require. Do not wait for a saturation collapse or diagnose OHS from
the change without further evidence.

124
00:25:02,664 --> 00:25:13,800
Reevaluate the movement plan with the additional airway needs. The
team must protect effective care throughout extraction rather than
prioritize finishing the original route unchanged.

125
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:26,328
Report what changed, when and from which baseline, including the
patient's usual mobility and reported respiratory support. Avoid using
bariatric patient as the entire clinical description.

126
00:25:26,328 --> 00:25:37,680
Distinguish reliable measurements from the questionable cuff reading
and limited auscultation. Describe the new drowsiness and shallow
breathing even though saturation increased.

127
00:25:37,680 --> 00:25:48,432
Explain care and its actual effect, including ventilation and
positioning. A list of equipment without a response does not show
whether the immediate threat improved.

128
00:25:48,432 --> 00:26:00,192
Communicate required movement support and unresolved gaps so the
receiving team can prepare. Preserve privacy and clinical relevance
when sharing size or equipment information.

129
00:26:00,192 --> 00:26:12,984
You should now connect the school mechanisms to observable findings
without diagnosing from size. Distinguish OSA from OHS and technical
ECG problems from an unexplained true small signal.

130
00:26:12,984 --> 00:26:24,408
Explain how cuff fit, access, skin folds and difficult sounds affect
certainty. Missing information must stay visible rather than become a
confident normal examination.

131
00:26:24,408 --> 00:26:38,016
In supervised practice, demonstrate appropriate ramping, the movement
assessment and use of the actual equipment. State capacity, route,
team roles and stop criteria before movement.

132
00:26:38,016 --> 00:26:49,728
Finally, show how reassessment and a respectful handover protect the
whole plan. The next session applies assessment modifications to
pregnancy and obstetric red flags.
